RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on the IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException — add @Blocking

## RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on the IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException — add @Blocking

### Symptom

A RESTEasy Reactive endpoint that unwraps a reactive result with `.await().atMost(TIMEOUT)` works fine in tests (low concurrency, small H2 responses) but throws `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` in production under load. The exception is thrown inside the Vert.x IO thread guard.

### Context

This surfaces when switching from a blocking API (returning `List<T>` directly) to a reactive API (returning `Uni<List<T>>`), and adapting by calling `.await()`:

```java
// quarkus-rest-jackson (RESTEasy Reactive) — IO thread by default
@GET
@Path("/channels")
public List<ChannelDetail> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels().await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10));  // ← blocks IO thread
}
```

The endpoint returns `List<T>` (not `Uni<T>` or `Multi<T>`), so RESTEasy Reactive dispatches it on the Vert.x IO thread. The `.await()` call then blocks that IO thread, which Vert.x prohibits.

### Root cause

RESTEasy Reactive runs endpoint methods on the Vert.x IO event loop by default. Blocking the event loop — even for a short duration — starves all other I/O operations. Vert.x detects this and throws `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException`.

The original blocking API (e.g. `QhorusMcpTools.listChannels()` returning `List<T>`) had the same structural problem but may not have surfaced it depending on the CDI dispatch context. Switching to a reactive API and calling `.await()` makes the blocking explicit and the guard fires.

### Fix

**Short-term:** Annotate blocking endpoints with `@Blocking` so RESTEasy Reactive dispatches them to a worker thread:

```java
@GET
@Path("/channels")
@Blocking   // io.smallrye.common.annotation.Blocking
public List<ChannelDetail> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels().await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10));
}
```

**Proper fix:** Return `Uni<T>` directly and let RESTEasy Reactive subscribe on the IO thread without blocking:

```java
@GET
@Path("/channels")
public Uni<List<ChannelDetail>> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels();
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

- Classic `quarkus-resteasy` dispatches all endpoints to worker threads — `.await()` works there without annotation.
- The endpoint compiles, starts, and passes unit/integration tests because test HTTP clients are synchronous and concurrency is low.
- The `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` only surfaces under real Vert.x IO thread dispatch — which requires `quarkus-rest` or `quarkus-resteasy-reactive` and actual concurrent load.
- The error message points to the `.await()` call site, but the root cause is the missing `@Blocking` or return-type change on the endpoint.

**See also:** [GE-20260526-399a43](GE-20260526-399a43.md) — quarkus-rest + JDBC Panache requires @Blocking — tests pass without it, production degrades silently

**See also:** [GE-20260617-36c6b5](GE-20260617-36c6b5.md) — @Blocking required on quarkus-rest JAX-RS handlers with polling loops | [GE-20260617-c2ceb3](GE-20260617-c2ceb3.md) — @Transactional on RESTEasy Reactive void SSE method

@Transactional(SUPPORTS) makes JPA reads callable from any thread — including Vert.x IO thread

## Context

When a JPA-backed method is called from a Vert.x IO thread (e.g. by a library that invokes a CDI bean from an event-bus handler without `@Blocking`), the default `@Transactional(REQUIRED)` annotation causes:

```
io.quarkus.runtime.BlockingOperationNotAllowedException:
  Cannot start a JTA transaction from the IO thread.
```

## Technique

Change read-only JPA methods from `@Transactional` (which defaults to `REQUIRED`) to `@Transactional(TxType.SUPPORTS)`:

```java
import jakarta.transaction.Transactional;
import jakarta.transaction.Transactional.TxType;

@Override
@Transactional(TxType.SUPPORTS)
public List<WorkerSummary> findCompletedWorkers(UUID caseId) {
    // SELECT query — safe to run without an active transaction.
    return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT e FROM CaseLedgerEntry e WHERE e.caseId = :caseId ...",
            CaseLedgerEntry.class)
        .setParameter("caseId", caseId)
        .getResultList();
}
```

`SUPPORTS` semantics:
- **With active transaction:** joins it (participates, sees uncommitted data in the same transaction)
- **Without active transaction:** proceeds without one — no JTA begin/end, no IO-thread check triggered

This is correct and safe for read-only queries: SELECTs work without a transaction in READ_COMMITTED isolation (each statement sees the latest committed state). If the same method is also called inside a `@TestTransaction`, it correctly joins that transaction.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Most developers annotate JPA methods with `@Transactional` (REQUIRED) as a blanket rule, or not at all (relying on the persistence context). The distinction between `REQUIRED` and `SUPPORTS` for read-only methods is easy to overlook, and the failure mode — `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` from inside a library call chain — doesn't obviously point to the `@Transactional` annotation on your own method.

**See also:** [GE-20260428-a67806](GE-20260428-a67806.md) — casehub-engine Vert.x event-bus handlers lack @Blocking — JPA consumer calls fail from IO thread

@Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

# @Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

## The technique

Add `@Startup` to any `@ApplicationScoped` bean whose `@PostConstruct` makes a blocking call:

```java
@Startup           // forces eager init on main thread during boot
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyClient {

@PostConstruct
    void validate() {
        // blocking: process probe, network check, file I/O, etc.
        new ProcessBuilder("claude", "--version").start().waitFor(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

Without `@Startup`, Quarkus ARC initializes `@ApplicationScoped` beans **lazily** — on first injection. If the first injection happens from a reactive handler (inside a Vert.x event-loop callback, a REST endpoint, a CDI observer), the `@PostConstruct` runs on the Vert.x IO thread. Any blocking call there violates the non-blocking contract and either deadlocks under load or triggers a `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException`.

`@Startup` forces initialization during application boot on the main thread, before any reactive handlers are active.

## Why this is non-obvious

`@Startup` is documented as "run initialization at startup" but the docs frame it in terms of *when* (boot vs. first use), not *which thread*. The connection to IO-thread safety is not mentioned. Developers who know about `@Startup` typically use it for "I want this to run at boot time" — not as a reactive-safety mechanism.

The failure mode is intermittent: `@PostConstruct` is safe when the first injection happens from a non-reactive context (tests, CLI, HTTP request on a worker thread). It only fails when the first injection happens under reactive load — making it a production-only bug in many setups.

## Codebase pattern

Same pattern used by `PathParserConfigurator` and `MongoPreferenceIndexes` in casehub-platform. Canonical for any bean that initialises eagerly and blocks during `@PostConstruct`.

## Why non-obvious

The standard mental model for `@ApplicationScoped` is "singleton, available everywhere, initialized once." The lazy-init detail — and its thread-safety implications in a reactive runtime — is not in the CDI spec, only in Quarkus ARC internals. A developer migrating from Spring (`@Bean` methods always run on the startup thread) would have no reason to anticipate the problem.

**See also:** [GE-20260522-daca26](GE-20260522-daca26.md) — Mutiny MultiEmitter.emit() from Vert.x I/O thread silently drops SSE frames | [GE-20260505-da346d](GE-20260505-da346d.md) — @ApplicationScoped CDI beans are always-active in Quarkus

**See also:** [GE-20260605-e42cc5](GE-20260605-e42cc5.md) — @Blocking on plain @ApplicationScoped method causes Quarkus test classloader failure

**See also:** [GE-20260607-cedf69](GE-20260607-cedf69.md) — @Startup @ApplicationScoped does not auto-invoke arbitrary methods — requires @PostConstruct

**See also:** [GE-20260615-d065bf](GE-20260615-d065bf.md) — two @Startup @ApplicationScoped beans have no guaranteed initialization order

emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool()) — correct way to shift blocking I/O off Vert.x IO thread in a reactive SPI

## Context

A reactive SPI method returns `Uni<T>` and needs to make a blocking call (e.g. HTTP to an embedding service, JDBC, file I/O). The SPI is called from a `@ConsumeEvent` handler (Vert.x event bus) which runs on the Vert.x IO thread unless `blocking = true` is set.

## Technique

Use `.emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` on the `Uni` **before** the blocking operation. This shifts the execution to the Mutiny default worker pool:

```java
@Override
public Uni<AgentAssignment> select(AgentRoutingContext context, List<AgentCandidate> candidates) {
    // Classify (in-memory, fast — can stay on caller thread)
    List<ClassifiedCandidate> classified = classifier.classify(candidates, ...);

return Uni.createFrom()
        .voidItem()
        .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())  // shift here, before blocking work
        .map(ignored -> {
            // Everything from here runs on the worker pool
            float[] queryVector = embeddingProvider.embed(queryText);  // blocking HTTP call
            // ... build scored candidates ...
            return TrustCandidateClassifier.decide(classified, scored, capability);
        });
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Three common but wrong alternatives:

**1. `Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blockingOperation())`**
Executes the supplier on the thread that subscribes (the IO thread). Still blocks if called from a `@ConsumeEvent` handler.

**2. `@Blocking` on the handler method**
Moves the *entire* handler to a worker thread, including all in-memory work before the SPI call. Wasteful and scatters the threading decision into the caller rather than the SPI implementation.

**3. `Uni.createFrom().emitter(em -> { em.complete(blocking()); })`**
Also executes on the subscribing thread unless `.emitOn()` is chained.

The correct model: the `Uni` pipeline is lazy. `.emitOn(workerPool)` inserts a thread-handoff at that point in the pipeline — everything downstream of it executes on the worker pool when the `Uni` is subscribed. Place it immediately before the first blocking call, after any fast in-memory preparation.

## Constraints

Requires `io.smallrye.mutiny:mutiny` and `io.smallrye.mutiny.infrastructure.Infrastructure` on the classpath (shipped with Quarkus). `Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool()` is the standard Mutiny executor for blocking work — do not pass a custom executor unless you need isolation.

**See also:** [GE-20260529-b994c2](GE-20260529-b994c2.md) — Uni.createFrom().item(supplier) with emitOn(): supplier still runs on subscription thread (use runSubscriptionOn instead)

CDI @PostConstruct JPA calls fail silently on Vert.x IO thread when engine's registerKnownDefinitions triggers lazy bean init

### Symptom

Quarkus startup fails with `BlockingNotAllowedException` or `ContextNotActiveException` from a `@PostConstruct` method that calls `ChannelService.findByName()` (JPA/Panache). The stack trace shows the call originates from `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry.registerKnownDefinitions()` running on a Vert.x IO thread.

### Context

When `QuarkMindCaseHub extends CaseHub` is discovered by CDI, the engine's `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry` calls `registerKnownDefinitions()` on a Vert.x IO thread during startup event processing. This triggers lazy resolution of CDI beans injected by the CaseHub subclass (e.g. `Instance<TaskDefinition>`). If any of those beans have `@PostConstruct` methods that perform blocking JPA work (channel creation, preference loading), the call hits the Vert.x IO thread blocker.

### Root cause

Vert.x IO threads prohibit blocking operations. JPA/Panache calls are blocking. The engine's case definition registration happens on an IO thread, which transitively constructs `@ApplicationScoped` beans that were designed to initialize eagerly via `@PostConstruct`.

### Fix

Defer blocking work from `@PostConstruct` to lazy initialization with double-checked locking:

```java
// Before (fails on IO thread):
@PostConstruct
void init() {
    channel = channelService.findByName("my-channel")
        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...));
}

// After (lazy, thread-safe):
private volatile UUID channelId;

private void ensureChannel() {
    if (channelId == null) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (channelId == null) {
                channelId = QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()
                    .call(() -> channelService.findByName("my-channel")
                        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...)))
                    .id();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call `ensureChannel()` on first use (e.g. first `execute()` call) instead of in `@PostConstruct`.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@PostConstruct` pattern is standard for CDI bean initialization. It works for all non-JPA work. The failure only occurs when the bean is transitively discovered by the engine's CaseDefinitionRegistry — a dependency chain that crosses module boundaries and isn't visible from the bean's own code.

quarkus-rest (RESTEasy Reactive) + JDBC Panache requires @Blocking on resource classes — tests pass without it, production degrades silently

### Symptom
`@QuarkusTest` integration tests pass with no errors or warnings. Application boots and serves requests correctly. Under production load, throughput degrades and latency spikes — or Quarkus throws `BlockingNotAllowedException` if the reactive thread-check is enabled.

No compiler warning. No test failure. No startup error. The problem is only visible under load or with explicit reactive thread checking enabled.

### Context
Occurs in any Quarkus application using `quarkus-rest` (RESTEasy Reactive) where REST resource methods call JDBC Panache (blocking ORM). `quarkus-rest` runs request handling on the Vert.x I/O thread by default. JDBC operations block the thread. Without `@Blocking`, the I/O thread is blocked for the duration of each database call.

`@QuarkusTest` does not enforce reactive thread discipline — tests pass regardless of whether `@Blocking` is declared. This gives false confidence that the configuration is correct.

### What was tried
Writing `@QuarkusTest` integration tests for REST resources with Panache calls → all tests pass. Deploying to production without `@Blocking` → event loop I/O thread blocked on each JDBC call, degrading throughput.

### Root cause
`quarkus-rest` (RESTEasy Reactive) uses the Vert.x event loop by default. `quarkus-hibernate-orm-panache` uses JDBC, which is inherently blocking. Without `@Blocking` on the resource class or method, Panache calls execute on the Vert.x I/O thread. Vert.x's design principle is that the I/O thread must never block — blocking it prevents it from handling other requests simultaneously.

`@QuarkusTest` does not reproduce the reactive thread model faithfully — it runs tests in a way that tolerates blocking calls on the I/O thread, so the violation is invisible during testing.

### Fix

Add `@Blocking` at the class level to all REST resource classes that call JDBC Panache:

```java
import io.smallrye.common.annotation.Blocking;

@Blocking                    // dispatches all methods to a worker thread
@ApplicationScoped
@Path("/household-tasks")
public class HouseholdTaskResource {

@Inject HouseholdTaskService service;

@GET
    public List<HouseholdTask> list() {
        return service.list();  // safe — runs on worker thread
    }
}
```

`@Blocking` at the class level applies to all methods. Individual methods can override with `@NonBlocking` if they don't need I/O.

**Alternative — virtual threads (Java 21+):**

```java
import io.smallrye.common.annotation.RunOnVirtualThread;

@RunOnVirtualThread          // runs each request on a virtual thread (non-blocking carrier)
@ApplicationScoped
@Path("/household-tasks")
public class HouseholdTaskResource { ... }
```

Virtual threads avoid blocking the carrier thread and scale better than traditional worker threads for I/O-bound workloads.

### Why this is non-obvious
`quarkus-resteasy` (classic) and `quarkus-resteasy-reactive` are easy to confuse. Classic runs on worker threads by default — `@Blocking` is not needed. Reactive (`quarkus-rest`) runs on I/O threads — `@Blocking` is required. The artifact name `quarkus-rest` does not signal "reactive", so developers familiar with classic Quarkus miss this.

Tests pass because `@QuarkusTest` does not enforce reactive thread discipline. The failure is only reproducible under concurrent production load or with Vert.x blocking check explicitly enabled. A developer can build and test a full application, ship it to production, and discover the issue only when load increases.

**See also:** [GE-20260518-e4fa52](GE-20260518-e4fa52.md) — RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException

Panache.withTransaction() requires a duplicated Vert.x context — executeBlocking() root context still fails VertxContextSafetyToggle

### Symptom

Error changes from "No current Vertx context found" to "Can't get the context safety flag: the current context is not a duplicated context" — wrapping the call in `vertx.executeBlocking()` seemed to fix the first error but the operation still fails.

### Context

Triggered when calling `Panache.withTransaction()` (or `withSession()`) from a `@BeforeEach` test method or other code that isn't on a proper Quarkus-managed request thread. Adding `vertx.executeBlocking()` provides *a* Vert.x context but not the right kind.

### Root cause

`SessionOperations.vertxContext()` in Quarkus Hibernate Reactive checks two things:
1. A Vert.x context exists (`Vertx.currentContext()`)
2. `VertxContextSafetyToggle.checkIsSafe()` — the context must be a *duplicated* context (`context.duplicate()`)

Worker threads from `vertx.executeBlocking()` run in a root context. Root contexts fail the safety check even though they are valid Vert.x contexts.

### Fix

**Option 1 — `@Blocking` on endpoint/method (production code):**
```java
@GET
@Path("/{id}/lineage")
@Blocking
public Response getLineage(@PathParam("id") String id) {
    return Response.ok(service.findAll().await().indefinitely()).build();
}
```
Quarkus creates a properly duplicated context for `@Blocking` method invocations.

**Option 2 — `@RunOnVertxContext` for test lifecycle methods:**
```java
@BeforeEach
@RunOnVertxContext
Uni<Void> setup() {
    return service.create("test-channel", ...).replaceWithVoid();
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

Two different error messages for two related but distinct constraints, encountered sequentially. A developer who added `executeBlocking()` to fix the first "No Vert.x context" error reasonably believes they are on the right track — the error did change. The duplicated-context requirement is not documented in `Panache.withTransaction()` JavaDoc and only appears via the internal safety toggle.

**See also:** [GE-20260529-78ecb9](GE-20260529-78ecb9.md) — @QuarkusTest methods calling Panache.withTransaction() on JUnit thread fail with 'No current Vertx context'

**See also:** [GE-20260616-312ba1](GE-20260616-312ba1.md) — @WithSession requires a safe Vert.x sub-context | [GE-20260616-99484f](GE-20260616-99484f.md) — Uni.combine() technique for concurrent blocking + reactive

Uni.createFrom().item(supplier) with emitOn() — supplier still runs on the subscription thread

## Context

A reactive SPI bridge wraps a blocking delegate using `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)`. The goal is to offload the blocking call off the Vert.x IO thread. `.emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` is chained expecting it to shift execution.

## Symptom

Vert.x blocked-thread warning fires in production. The supplier still executes on the event loop thread despite the `emitOn` chain.

## Root Cause

`emitOn(executor)` controls where **downstream signals** (item, failure, completion) are dispatched to subscribers — not where the upstream computation runs. For `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)`, the supplier is called during subscription, on the subscription thread. If the subscription originates on the IO thread, `emitOn` has no effect on when the supplier runs — by the time the signal reaches `emitOn`, the supplier has already executed and blocked.

The correct method is `runSubscriptionOn(executor)`, which routes the subscription request itself to the given executor — meaning the supplier in `item()` evaluates on that executor.

## Fix

```java
// ❌ Wrong — emitOn does not offload the item() supplier
return Uni.createFrom()
    .item(() -> delegate.render(descriptor, context))
    .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool());

// ✅ Correct — runSubscriptionOn routes the subscription (and the supplier) to the worker pool
return Uni.createFrom()
    .item(() -> delegate.render(descriptor, context))
    .runSubscriptionOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool());
```

The `runSubscriptionOn` fix applies to any `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` wrapping a blocking call. `emitOn` is correct when the blocking work is in a downstream operator that follows it (e.g. `.voidItem().emitOn(pool).map(ignored -> blockingWork())`).

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

`emitOn` and `runSubscriptionOn` look symmetrical — both redirect to an executor — but they operate at different points in the Mutiny pipeline. The Vert.x blocked-thread warning is the only signal something is wrong; there is no compile-time error. The existing technique GE-20260529-ff186e shows `emitOn` working correctly for a different pattern; this gotcha explains when it silently fails.

### Why this fix

`runSubscriptionOn` is the right semantic: "evaluate this on the worker pool", which is exactly what's needed for a bridge that wraps a blocking operation in a supplier. `emitOn` is the right semantic for "deliver results to consumers on this executor" — a different concern.

## Constraints

Applies to `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` and `Uni.createFrom().item(value)`. For `Uni.createFrom().emitter(...)` or operator chains, `emitOn` placed before the blocking operator is correct.

## Update — CDI request-scoped beans unavailable inside the item() supplier

Because `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` executes the supplier at subscription time (on the
subscription thread), any CDI `@RequestScoped` bean accessed inside the lambda will throw
`ContextNotActiveException` if the subscription happens on a non-request thread (e.g. a Vert.x
worker thread or Quarkus managed executor).

**Common case:** `CurrentPrincipal` is `@RequestScoped`. Injecting it into an
`@ApplicationScoped` reactive service and reading it inside the Uni lambda is safe only
if the subscription always originates on a request thread. If the caller schedules
the Uni on a worker thread (e.g. via `@Blocking` or `runSubscriptionOn`), the CDI
request scope is no longer active and the read throws.

**Fix:** Capture any `@RequestScoped` value as a local variable BEFORE the Uni is
constructed — on the calling (request) thread — then use the captured value inside
the lambda:

```java
// ❌ Wrong — reads CurrentPrincipal inside the lambda (may run off request thread)
@Inject CurrentPrincipal principal;

return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> {
    String tenancyId = principal.tenancyId();  // ContextNotActiveException if not on request thread
    return service.process(tenancyId);
});

// ✅ Correct — capture on the calling (request) thread before the Uni
String tenancyId = principal.tenancyId();  // safe: called on request thread
return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> {
    return service.process(tenancyId);       // captured local — no CDI scope dependency
});
```

This applies to any `@RequestScoped` bean: `CurrentPrincipal`, HTTP headers via
`@Context HttpHeaders`, security context, etc.

## Variant — Vert.x timer re-dispatch (nested runSubscriptionOn inside flatMap)

When a fault handler uses a Vert.x timer for backoff delay and then re-dispatches
via `flatMap(submit)`, the `submit()` method's `runSubscriptionOn` only affects the
inner subscription. The `flatMap` itself and any operations before the inner Uni
execute on the timer's callback thread — which is the Vert.x event loop.

```java
// ❌ Wrong — submit() uses runSubscriptionOn internally, but the flatMap
// chain before the inner subscription runs on the timer's event-loop thread
return Uni.createFrom().<Void>emitter(em -> {
        long timerId = vertx.setTimer(delayMs, id -> em.complete(null));
        em.onTermination(() -> vertx.cancelTimer(timerId));
    })
    .flatMap(ignored -> workerExecutionManager.submit(...));
    // submit().runSubscriptionOn protects the inner supplier,
    // but initial chain operations may run on the event loop

// ✅ Correct — emitOn(workerPool) before flatMap ensures the entire
// chain after the timer fires on the worker pool
return Uni.createFrom().<Void>emitter(em -> {
        long timerId = vertx.setTimer(delayMs, id -> em.complete(null));
        em.onTermination(() -> vertx.cancelTimer(timerId));
    })
    .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())
    .flatMap(ignored -> workerExecutionManager.submit(...));
```

@Blocking rejected on @ApplicationScoped CDI bean methods returning Uni — only valid on framework entrypoints

## Symptom

Build fails with an error from `ExecutionModelAnnotationsProcessor` when `@Blocking` is placed
on an `@ApplicationScoped` CDI bean method that returns `Uni<T>` or `Uni<Void>`.

## Context

Adding `@Blocking` to a bridge or wrapper class that wraps blocking delegate calls and exposes
them as `Uni<>`. The intent: tell Quarkus to dispatch the blocking code to a worker thread.

```java
@DefaultBean @ApplicationScoped
public class BlockingToReactiveBridge implements ReactiveCaseMemoryStore {

@Inject CaseMemoryStore delegate;

@Override @Blocking   // ← build error here
    public Uni<String> store(MemoryInput input) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> delegate.store(input));
    }
}
```

Build output (from `ExecutionModelAnnotationsProcessor`):
```
Build step io.quarkus.arc.processor.BeanProcessor#build threw an exception:
io.quarkus.deployment.exception.BuildException: Build failure
@Blocking, @NonBlocking and @RunOnVirtualThread are not supported on non-entrypoint methods
```

## Root Cause

Quarkus's `ExecutionModelAnnotationsProcessor` only recognises `@Blocking`, `@NonBlocking`,
and `@RunOnVirtualThread` on *framework entrypoints* — JAX-RS / Quarkus REST resource methods,
reactive messaging `@Incoming` consumers, GraphQL resolvers, and similar. On a plain
`@ApplicationScoped` CDI bean method — even one returning `Uni` — the annotation is rejected
at build time with a hard error.

The annotation is not a general hint to the CDI container. It is a deployment-time instruction
to specific framework processors that know how to change the thread dispatch model for their
own entry points.

## Fix

Remove `@Blocking` from CDI bean methods. Thread dispatch is the responsibility of the
*entrypoint* (e.g. a `@Blocking`-annotated JAX-RS method) calling into the CDI bean.

For a bridge that wraps blocking code as `Uni`, the `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` pattern
already defers execution until subscription — the subscriber's thread model determines where
the supplier runs. For void blocking calls, `Uni.createFrom().voidItem().invoke(action)` is
the idiomatic Mutiny pattern.

```java
@DefaultBean @ApplicationScoped
public class BlockingToReactiveBridge implements ReactiveCaseMemoryStore {

@Inject CaseMemoryStore delegate;

@Override  // no @Blocking — entrypoint is responsible for thread dispatch
    public Uni<String> store(MemoryInput input) {
        return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> delegate.store(input));
    }

@Override
    public Uni<Void> erase(EraseRequest request) {
        return Uni.createFrom().voidItem().invoke(() -> delegate.erase(request));
    }
}
```

If the CDI bean itself must guarantee worker-thread execution (not relying on the caller),
use `runSubscriptionOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` on the returned `Uni` instead.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The Quarkus documentation says to use `@Blocking` when a method blocks. Nothing in the
documentation limits this to framework entrypoints — the restriction is only discoverable by
hitting the build error or reading the `ExecutionModelAnnotationsProcessor` source. Developers
familiar with `@Blocking @Tool` on MCP handlers or `@Blocking @Incoming` on messaging consumers
will naturally apply it to any CDI bean method they write that wraps blocking code.

**See also:** [GE-20260602-093fea](GE-20260602-093fea.md) — @Blocking on plain CDI @ApplicationScoped service methods is invalid (more general case)

**See also:** [GE-20260605-e42cc5](GE-20260605-e42cc5.md) — @Blocking on plain @ApplicationScoped method causes test classloader failure (different scenario)

JEP SharedInterpreter called from non-owner thread hangs indefinitely — no exception

### Symptom
HTTP requests to a Quarkus server hang indefinitely — no error, no exception, no timeout. The server log shows the request was received but never responds. A JVM thread dump shows HTTP worker threads blocked inside JEP native code.

### Context
Using JEP `SharedInterpreter` in a `@ApplicationScoped` Quarkus bean. The interpreter is created in `@PostConstruct` (on a Quarkus startup thread). HTTP requests arrive on Vert.x worker threads. All `interp.invoke()` calls are guarded with `synchronized` to prevent concurrent access.

### What was tried
`synchronized` on the bridge method — prevents concurrent calls but does **not** fix the underlying issue. The first HTTP thread to acquire the lock hangs forever.

### Root cause
JEP's `SharedInterpreter` has **thread affinity**: it must be used exclusively from the thread that created it. When a different thread (an HTTP worker) calls `interp.invoke()`, JEP attempts to acquire the GIL from the wrong thread. The owner thread (startup thread, long since idle) effectively holds the GIL state; the caller waits forever. This is a **deadlock, not an exception** — JEP does not throw; it simply never returns.

`synchronized` prevents two HTTP threads from calling JEP simultaneously, but it does not transfer thread ownership. Any single HTTP thread calling JEP is already the wrong thread.

### Fix
Run a dedicated daemon thread that owns the `SharedInterpreter` for its entire lifetime. All callers submit tasks via a `LinkedBlockingQueue` and wait on a `CompletableFuture` with a timeout:

```java
private record Task(String function, Object[] args, CompletableFuture<String> result) {}
private final LinkedBlockingQueue<Task> queue = new LinkedBlockingQueue<>();

@PostConstruct
void init() {
    CompletableFuture<String> initDone = new CompletableFuture<>();
    Thread t = new Thread(() -> runLoop(initDone), "python-bridge");
    t.setDaemon(true);
    t.start();
    initDone.get(60, TimeUnit.SECONDS); // blocks until Python is ready
}

private void runLoop(CompletableFuture<String> initDone) {
    SharedInterpreter interp = new SharedInterpreter();
    // ... Python initialisation ...
    initDone.complete(initResult);

while (!Thread.currentThread().isInterrupted()) {
        Task task = queue.poll(5, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
        if (task == null) continue;
        try {
            task.result().complete((String) interp.invoke(task.function(), task.args()));
        } catch (JepException e) {
            task.result().complete(errorJson(e.getMessage()));
        }
    }
}

public String call(String function, Object... args) {
    CompletableFuture<String> future = new CompletableFuture<>();
    queue.offer(new Task(function, args, future));
    return future.get(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS); // timeout prevents runaway hangs
}
```

The 30-second timeout on `call()` also ensures test suites don't hang forever if Python gets stuck.

### Why this is non-obvious
`synchronized` is the standard Java solution for "only one thread at a time." It correctly serialises calls. But JEP's thread affinity is not a *concurrency* constraint — it is an *ownership* constraint. The fix requires a different architecture (dedicated thread + message passing), not a stronger lock.

The JEP README says `SharedInterpreter` "should only be used from the thread it was created on," which reads as a soft style recommendation. In practice, calling from the wrong thread does not raise an exception — it deadlocks silently, making diagnosis very difficult.

### Why this fix
A single dedicated thread serialises naturally and satisfies thread affinity with no locking overhead. The task-queue pattern also centralises error handling and makes the timeout boundary explicit.

### Alternatives considered
- `synchronized` — prevents concurrent calls but doesn't fix thread ownership; still hangs
- Thread-local `SubInterpreter` — each HTTP thread gets its own interpreter; avoids affinity but multiple Python GILs interact unpredictably and state is not shared across requests
- `Py4J` sidecar — separate Python process over sockets; solves threading but adds 100–200ms per call and process management complexity

**See also:** GE-20260415-b218d7 [Dedicated daemon thread + task queue for thread-affinite interpreters in multi-threaded JVM servers]

@Transactional(SUPPORTS) makes JPA reads callable from any thread — including Vert.x IO thread

## Context

When a JPA-backed method is called from a Vert.x IO thread (e.g. by a library that invokes a CDI bean from an event-bus handler without `@Blocking`), the default `@Transactional(REQUIRED)` annotation causes:

```
io.quarkus.runtime.BlockingOperationNotAllowedException:
  Cannot start a JTA transaction from the IO thread.
```

## Technique

Change read-only JPA methods from `@Transactional` (which defaults to `REQUIRED`) to `@Transactional(TxType.SUPPORTS)`:

```java
import jakarta.transaction.Transactional;
import jakarta.transaction.Transactional.TxType;

@Override
@Transactional(TxType.SUPPORTS)
public List<WorkerSummary> findCompletedWorkers(UUID caseId) {
    // SELECT query — safe to run without an active transaction.
    return em.createQuery(
            "SELECT e FROM CaseLedgerEntry e WHERE e.caseId = :caseId ...",
            CaseLedgerEntry.class)
        .setParameter("caseId", caseId)
        .getResultList();
}
```

`SUPPORTS` semantics:
- **With active transaction:** joins it (participates, sees uncommitted data in the same transaction)
- **Without active transaction:** proceeds without one — no JTA begin/end, no IO-thread check triggered

This is correct and safe for read-only queries: SELECTs work without a transaction in READ_COMMITTED isolation (each statement sees the latest committed state). If the same method is also called inside a `@TestTransaction`, it correctly joins that transaction.

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Most developers annotate JPA methods with `@Transactional` (REQUIRED) as a blanket rule, or not at all (relying on the persistence context). The distinction between `REQUIRED` and `SUPPORTS` for read-only methods is easy to overlook, and the failure mode — `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` from inside a library call chain — doesn't obviously point to the `@Transactional` annotation on your own method.

**See also:** [GE-20260428-a67806](GE-20260428-a67806.md) — casehub-engine Vert.x event-bus handlers lack @Blocking — JPA consumer calls fail from IO thread

RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on the IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException — add @Blocking

## RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on the IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException — add @Blocking

### Symptom

A RESTEasy Reactive endpoint that unwraps a reactive result with `.await().atMost(TIMEOUT)` works fine in tests (low concurrency, small H2 responses) but throws `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` in production under load. The exception is thrown inside the Vert.x IO thread guard.

### Context

This surfaces when switching from a blocking API (returning `List<T>` directly) to a reactive API (returning `Uni<List<T>>`), and adapting by calling `.await()`:

```java
// quarkus-rest-jackson (RESTEasy Reactive) — IO thread by default
@GET
@Path("/channels")
public List<ChannelDetail> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels().await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10));  // ← blocks IO thread
}
```

The endpoint returns `List<T>` (not `Uni<T>` or `Multi<T>`), so RESTEasy Reactive dispatches it on the Vert.x IO thread. The `.await()` call then blocks that IO thread, which Vert.x prohibits.

### Root cause

RESTEasy Reactive runs endpoint methods on the Vert.x IO event loop by default. Blocking the event loop — even for a short duration — starves all other I/O operations. Vert.x detects this and throws `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException`.

The original blocking API (e.g. `QhorusMcpTools.listChannels()` returning `List<T>`) had the same structural problem but may not have surfaced it depending on the CDI dispatch context. Switching to a reactive API and calling `.await()` makes the blocking explicit and the guard fires.

### Fix

**Short-term:** Annotate blocking endpoints with `@Blocking` so RESTEasy Reactive dispatches them to a worker thread:

```java
@GET
@Path("/channels")
@Blocking   // io.smallrye.common.annotation.Blocking
public List<ChannelDetail> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels().await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(10));
}
```

**Proper fix:** Return `Uni<T>` directly and let RESTEasy Reactive subscribe on the IO thread without blocking:

```java
@GET
@Path("/channels")
public Uni<List<ChannelDetail>> channels() {
    return reactiveService.listChannels();
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

- Classic `quarkus-resteasy` dispatches all endpoints to worker threads — `.await()` works there without annotation.
- The endpoint compiles, starts, and passes unit/integration tests because test HTTP clients are synchronous and concurrency is low.
- The `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException` only surfaces under real Vert.x IO thread dispatch — which requires `quarkus-rest` or `quarkus-resteasy-reactive` and actual concurrent load.
- The error message points to the `.await()` call site, but the root cause is the missing `@Blocking` or return-type change on the endpoint.

**See also:** [GE-20260526-399a43](GE-20260526-399a43.md) — quarkus-rest + JDBC Panache requires @Blocking — tests pass without it, production degrades silently

**See also:** [GE-20260617-36c6b5](GE-20260617-36c6b5.md) — @Blocking required on quarkus-rest JAX-RS handlers with polling loops | [GE-20260617-c2ceb3](GE-20260617-c2ceb3.md) — @Transactional on RESTEasy Reactive void SSE method

emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool()) — correct way to shift blocking I/O off Vert.x IO thread in a reactive SPI

## Context

A reactive SPI method returns `Uni<T>` and needs to make a blocking call (e.g. HTTP to an embedding service, JDBC, file I/O). The SPI is called from a `@ConsumeEvent` handler (Vert.x event bus) which runs on the Vert.x IO thread unless `blocking = true` is set.

## Technique

Use `.emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` on the `Uni` **before** the blocking operation. This shifts the execution to the Mutiny default worker pool:

```java
@Override
public Uni<AgentAssignment> select(AgentRoutingContext context, List<AgentCandidate> candidates) {
    // Classify (in-memory, fast — can stay on caller thread)
    List<ClassifiedCandidate> classified = classifier.classify(candidates, ...);

return Uni.createFrom()
        .voidItem()
        .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())  // shift here, before blocking work
        .map(ignored -> {
            // Everything from here runs on the worker pool
            float[] queryVector = embeddingProvider.embed(queryText);  // blocking HTTP call
            // ... build scored candidates ...
            return TrustCandidateClassifier.decide(classified, scored, capability);
        });
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Three common but wrong alternatives:

**1. `Uni.createFrom().item(() -> blockingOperation())`**
Executes the supplier on the thread that subscribes (the IO thread). Still blocks if called from a `@ConsumeEvent` handler.

**2. `@Blocking` on the handler method**
Moves the *entire* handler to a worker thread, including all in-memory work before the SPI call. Wasteful and scatters the threading decision into the caller rather than the SPI implementation.

**3. `Uni.createFrom().emitter(em -> { em.complete(blocking()); })`**
Also executes on the subscribing thread unless `.emitOn()` is chained.

The correct model: the `Uni` pipeline is lazy. `.emitOn(workerPool)` inserts a thread-handoff at that point in the pipeline — everything downstream of it executes on the worker pool when the `Uni` is subscribed. Place it immediately before the first blocking call, after any fast in-memory preparation.

## Constraints

Requires `io.smallrye.mutiny:mutiny` and `io.smallrye.mutiny.infrastructure.Infrastructure` on the classpath (shipped with Quarkus). `Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool()` is the standard Mutiny executor for blocking work — do not pass a custom executor unless you need isolation.

**See also:** [GE-20260529-b994c2](GE-20260529-b994c2.md) — Uni.createFrom().item(supplier) with emitOn(): supplier still runs on subscription thread (use runSubscriptionOn instead)

DB query over CaseInstanceCache for Vert.x @ConsumeEvent gate discrimination — race-free, restart-safe

### Context

A `@ConsumeEvent(blocking = true)` handler subscribes to `casehub.action.gate.rejected` (or approved/expired). Multiple case hub types share the same event bus address. The handler must determine whether this gate event belongs to its case type before writing a ledger entry or signalling the case.

### Problem with the obvious approach

The obvious approach — `CaseInstanceCache.get(event.caseId()).getPendingActionGate().plannedAction().actionType()` — has a non-deterministic race condition: the engine's gate handlers also subscribe to the same addresses and clear `pendingActionGate` synchronously. If the engine handler runs before the application consumer, `getPendingActionGate()` returns null. See GE-20260613-29d3b5.

### Technique

Store the case ID on a domain entity during the case start sequence (Phase 3 of the three-phase pattern). Query that entity by case ID in the `@ConsumeEvent` handler. Non-null = this gate belongs to this consumer.

**Domain entity setup (Phase 3):**

```java
@Transactional
void persistCaseId(UUID entityId, UUID caseId) {
    MyEntity entity = MyEntity.findById(entityId);
    entity.myCaseId = caseId;   // persisted before gate WorkItem is created
}
```

**Gate discriminator in the listener:**

```java
@ConsumeEvent(value = "casehub.action.gate.rejected", blocking = true)
public void onRejected(ActionGateRejectedEvent event) {
    MyEntity entity = MyEntity.findByCaseId(event.caseId());
    if (entity == null) return; // not our gate type

// entity.id, entity.enrollmentId, entity.grade etc. available here
    // no secondary lookups needed
    ledgerWriter.writeEntry(entity, "REJECTED", Instant.now(), event.rejectedBy());
    caseHub.signal(event.caseId(), "assessmentComplete", true);
}
```

**Why it works:**

- Phase 3 commits `myCaseId` before `startCase()` returns and before the engine creates the gate WorkItem
- By the time the gate event fires and the consumer runs, the DB row exists
- The Panache query is race-free — the DB is the source of truth, not an in-memory cache
- Survives JVM restart (unlike `CaseInstanceCache`, which is in-memory)

**Side benefit:** The entity provides all domain context fields (IDs, grade, status) without secondary cache or context lookups.

### Why this is non-obvious

Developers familiar with the engine naturally reach for `CaseInstanceCache` since it's injected and always returns the case instance. The race condition only manifests in production under load. The DB query approach feels indirect but is structurally correct.

**See also:** [GE-20260613-29d3b5](GE-20260613-29d3b5.md) — ActionGateRejectedHandler clears pendingActionGate before @ConsumeEvent application consumers run (the race condition this technique solves)

CDI @PostConstruct JPA calls fail silently on Vert.x IO thread when engine's registerKnownDefinitions triggers lazy bean init

### Symptom

Quarkus startup fails with `BlockingNotAllowedException` or `ContextNotActiveException` from a `@PostConstruct` method that calls `ChannelService.findByName()` (JPA/Panache). The stack trace shows the call originates from `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry.registerKnownDefinitions()` running on a Vert.x IO thread.

### Context

When `QuarkMindCaseHub extends CaseHub` is discovered by CDI, the engine's `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry` calls `registerKnownDefinitions()` on a Vert.x IO thread during startup event processing. This triggers lazy resolution of CDI beans injected by the CaseHub subclass (e.g. `Instance<TaskDefinition>`). If any of those beans have `@PostConstruct` methods that perform blocking JPA work (channel creation, preference loading), the call hits the Vert.x IO thread blocker.

### Root cause

Vert.x IO threads prohibit blocking operations. JPA/Panache calls are blocking. The engine's case definition registration happens on an IO thread, which transitively constructs `@ApplicationScoped` beans that were designed to initialize eagerly via `@PostConstruct`.

### Fix

Defer blocking work from `@PostConstruct` to lazy initialization with double-checked locking:

```java
// Before (fails on IO thread):
@PostConstruct
void init() {
    channel = channelService.findByName("my-channel")
        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...));
}

// After (lazy, thread-safe):
private volatile UUID channelId;

private void ensureChannel() {
    if (channelId == null) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (channelId == null) {
                channelId = QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()
                    .call(() -> channelService.findByName("my-channel")
                        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...)))
                    .id();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call `ensureChannel()` on first use (e.g. first `execute()` call) instead of in `@PostConstruct`.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@PostConstruct` pattern is standard for CDI bean initialization. It works for all non-JPA work. The failure only occurs when the bean is transitively discovered by the engine's CaseDefinitionRegistry — a dependency chain that crosses module boundaries and isn't visible from the bean's own code.

Uni.createFrom().item(supplier) with emitOn() — supplier still runs on the subscription thread

## Context

A reactive SPI bridge wraps a blocking delegate using `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)`. The goal is to offload the blocking call off the Vert.x IO thread. `.emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())` is chained expecting it to shift execution.

## Symptom

Vert.x blocked-thread warning fires in production. The supplier still executes on the event loop thread despite the `emitOn` chain.

## Root Cause

`emitOn(executor)` controls where **downstream signals** (item, failure, completion) are dispatched to subscribers — not where the upstream computation runs. For `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)`, the supplier is called during subscription, on the subscription thread. If the subscription originates on the IO thread, `emitOn` has no effect on when the supplier runs — by the time the signal reaches `emitOn`, the supplier has already executed and blocked.

The correct method is `runSubscriptionOn(executor)`, which routes the subscription request itself to the given executor — meaning the supplier in `item()` evaluates on that executor.

## Fix

```java
// ❌ Wrong — emitOn does not offload the item() supplier
return Uni.createFrom()
    .item(() -> delegate.render(descriptor, context))
    .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool());

// ✅ Correct — runSubscriptionOn routes the subscription (and the supplier) to the worker pool
return Uni.createFrom()
    .item(() -> delegate.render(descriptor, context))
    .runSubscriptionOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool());
```

The `runSubscriptionOn` fix applies to any `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` wrapping a blocking call. `emitOn` is correct when the blocking work is in a downstream operator that follows it (e.g. `.voidItem().emitOn(pool).map(ignored -> blockingWork())`).

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

`emitOn` and `runSubscriptionOn` look symmetrical — both redirect to an executor — but they operate at different points in the Mutiny pipeline. The Vert.x blocked-thread warning is the only signal something is wrong; there is no compile-time error. The existing technique GE-20260529-ff186e shows `emitOn` working correctly for a different pattern; this gotcha explains when it silently fails.

### Why this fix

`runSubscriptionOn` is the right semantic: "evaluate this on the worker pool", which is exactly what's needed for a bridge that wraps a blocking operation in a supplier. `emitOn` is the right semantic for "deliver results to consumers on this executor" — a different concern.

## Constraints

Applies to `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` and `Uni.createFrom().item(value)`. For `Uni.createFrom().emitter(...)` or operator chains, `emitOn` placed before the blocking operator is correct.

## Update — CDI request-scoped beans unavailable inside the item() supplier

Because `Uni.createFrom().item(supplier)` executes the supplier at subscription time (on the
subscription thread), any CDI `@RequestScoped` bean accessed inside the lambda will throw
`ContextNotActiveException` if the subscription happens on a non-request thread (e.g. a Vert.x
worker thread or Quarkus managed executor).

**Common case:** `CurrentPrincipal` is `@RequestScoped`. Injecting it into an
`@ApplicationScoped` reactive service and reading it inside the Uni lambda is safe only
if the subscription always originates on a request thread. If the caller schedules
the Uni on a worker thread (e.g. via `@Blocking` or `runSubscriptionOn`), the CDI
request scope is no longer active and the read throws.

**Fix:** Capture any `@RequestScoped` value as a local variable BEFORE the Uni is
constructed — on the calling (request) thread — then use the captured value inside
the lambda:

```java
// ❌ Wrong — reads CurrentPrincipal inside the lambda (may run off request thread)
@Inject CurrentPrincipal principal;

return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> {
    String tenancyId = principal.tenancyId();  // ContextNotActiveException if not on request thread
    return service.process(tenancyId);
});

// ✅ Correct — capture on the calling (request) thread before the Uni
String tenancyId = principal.tenancyId();  // safe: called on request thread
return Uni.createFrom().item(() -> {
    return service.process(tenancyId);       // captured local — no CDI scope dependency
});
```

This applies to any `@RequestScoped` bean: `CurrentPrincipal`, HTTP headers via
`@Context HttpHeaders`, security context, etc.

## Variant — Vert.x timer re-dispatch (nested runSubscriptionOn inside flatMap)

When a fault handler uses a Vert.x timer for backoff delay and then re-dispatches
via `flatMap(submit)`, the `submit()` method's `runSubscriptionOn` only affects the
inner subscription. The `flatMap` itself and any operations before the inner Uni
execute on the timer's callback thread — which is the Vert.x event loop.

```java
// ❌ Wrong — submit() uses runSubscriptionOn internally, but the flatMap
// chain before the inner subscription runs on the timer's event-loop thread
return Uni.createFrom().<Void>emitter(em -> {
        long timerId = vertx.setTimer(delayMs, id -> em.complete(null));
        em.onTermination(() -> vertx.cancelTimer(timerId));
    })
    .flatMap(ignored -> workerExecutionManager.submit(...));
    // submit().runSubscriptionOn protects the inner supplier,
    // but initial chain operations may run on the event loop

// ✅ Correct — emitOn(workerPool) before flatMap ensures the entire
// chain after the timer fires on the worker pool
return Uni.createFrom().<Void>emitter(em -> {
        long timerId = vertx.setTimer(delayMs, id -> em.complete(null));
        em.onTermination(() -> vertx.cancelTimer(timerId));
    })
    .emitOn(Infrastructure.getDefaultWorkerPool())
    .flatMap(ignored -> workerExecutionManager.submit(...));
```

@ConsumeEvent(blocking=true) and @Transactional on the same method work correctly in Quarkus

## Symptom / Discovery

A `@ConsumeEvent` handler needs to call `@Transactional` services *and* start its own transaction (rather than relying on each called service to start its own). The question is whether placing both `@ConsumeEvent(blocking=true)` and `@Transactional` on the same method is supported.

## Context

Quarkus dispatches `@ConsumeEvent(blocking=true)` handlers on a worker thread pool (not the Vert.x IO thread). CDI interceptors — including the `@Transactional` interceptor — apply to `@ApplicationScoped` beans by default. When the event bus invokes the handler method via the CDI proxy, the `@Transactional` interceptor wraps the call, starting a JTA transaction before the method body and committing or rolling back when it exits.

## What works

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyHandler {

@ConsumeEvent(value = "my-address", blocking = true)
    @Transactional
    public void handle(MyEvent event) {
        // All JPA/JTA operations here share one transaction.
        // If anything throws, the whole transaction rolls back.
        serviceA.create(...);  // joins this transaction (REQUIRED semantics)
        serviceB.update(...);  // joins this transaction
    }
}
```

The `@Transactional` annotation is applied by the CDI proxy when the event bus invokes the handler. Both annotations are active simultaneously.

## Why this is not in the docs

Quarkus documents `@ConsumeEvent` and `@Transactional` separately. The interaction between them on the same method — specifically that `blocking=true` is necessary for `@Transactional` to work correctly, and that combining both annotations is valid — is not explicitly documented. The CLAUDE.md convention for this codebase states: "`@ConsumeEvent` handlers that call `@Transactional` services must use `blocking=true`" but does not note that `@Transactional` on the handler itself is also supported.

## Verified behaviour

Tests using `@QuarkusTest` confirmed that a `@ConsumeEvent(blocking=true) @Transactional` handler:
- Starts a single JTA transaction for the full method body
- Rolls back all JPA writes (including multiple service calls) if any step throws
- Commits atomically when the method exits cleanly

**See also:** [GE-20260429-68ee24](GE-20260429-68ee24.md) — @ConsumeEvent handler silently deadlocks if .join() called without blocking=true

**See also:** [GE-20260529-e43076](GE-20260529-e43076.md) — await CDI fireAsync() delivery in @ConsumeEvent reactive chain

**See also:** [GE-20260604-d08c9f](GE-20260604-d08c9f.md) — @Transactional and @Tool can coexist on same method

@Blocking required on quarkus-rest JAX-RS handlers that contain polling loops — omitting it starves the event loop and blocks concurrent requests

### Context
A JAX-RS handler method contains a synchronous polling loop (`Thread.sleep()` + repeated JPA reads) that runs for an extended period (seconds to minutes). The handler is in an application using `quarkus-rest` (RESTEasy Reactive), not `quarkus-resteasy` (Classic).

### The non-obvious part
In `quarkus-rest`, JAX-RS handler methods run on the **Vert.x event loop thread** by default. A blocking call (`Thread.sleep`, synchronous JPA, synchronous HTTP) on the event loop blocks **all** other incoming HTTP requests — they queue behind it. A polling handler that runs for 3–5 minutes effectively takes the server offline for all other requests during that time.

In our case: `POST /example/.../start` (which contains the polling loop) was called, and then `POST /openclaw/delivery/oversight/{gateId}` (the approval endpoint that unblocks the polling loop) could never be processed — the event loop was occupied by the first request, deadlocking the demo.

### Fix
Annotate the handler method with `@Blocking`:
```java
import io.smallrye.common.annotation.Blocking;

@POST
@Path("/{exampleId}/start")
@Blocking   // moves execution to Quarkus worker thread pool
public Response start(@PathParam("exampleId") String exampleId) {
    // Thread.sleep() calls and JPA reads are safe here
}
```
`@Blocking` offloads the handler to the Quarkus worker thread pool, leaving the event loop free for all concurrent requests.

### Why this is non-obvious
`quarkus-rest` and `quarkus-resteasy` have different default execution models. In the classic stack, JAX-RS methods block by default. In the reactive stack, they do not. If you use a polling loop that worked fine with `quarkus-resteasy`, it silently breaks under `quarkus-rest`. The failure mode is not an error — it is a deadlock that only surfaces when a second concurrent request is needed to unblock the first.

Note: an existing garden entry (GE-20260428-a67806) covers `@Blocking` for Vert.x event-bus JPA failures — the issue there is JPA from an IO thread. This entry is specifically about **JAX-RS handlers with polling loops** creating a deadlock by starving concurrent HTTP requests.

**See also:** [GE-20260617-c2ceb3](GE-20260617-c2ceb3.md) — @Transactional on RESTEasy Reactive void SSE method is load-bearing for virtual-thread dispatch | [GE-20260518-e4fa52](GE-20260518-e4fa52.md) — RESTEasy Reactive endpoints that call .await() on IO thread throw BlockingOperationNotAllowedException | [GE-20260613-a5983e](GE-20260613-a5983e.md) — @Transactional void JAX-RS SSE commits when body returns

@Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

# @Startup @ApplicationScoped prevents blocking @PostConstruct from running on Vert.x IO thread

## The technique

Add `@Startup` to any `@ApplicationScoped` bean whose `@PostConstruct` makes a blocking call:

```java
@Startup           // forces eager init on main thread during boot
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyClient {

@PostConstruct
    void validate() {
        // blocking: process probe, network check, file I/O, etc.
        new ProcessBuilder("claude", "--version").start().waitFor(10, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
    }
}
```

Without `@Startup`, Quarkus ARC initializes `@ApplicationScoped` beans **lazily** — on first injection. If the first injection happens from a reactive handler (inside a Vert.x event-loop callback, a REST endpoint, a CDI observer), the `@PostConstruct` runs on the Vert.x IO thread. Any blocking call there violates the non-blocking contract and either deadlocks under load or triggers a `BlockingOperationNotAllowedException`.

`@Startup` forces initialization during application boot on the main thread, before any reactive handlers are active.

## Why this is non-obvious

`@Startup` is documented as "run initialization at startup" but the docs frame it in terms of *when* (boot vs. first use), not *which thread*. The connection to IO-thread safety is not mentioned. Developers who know about `@Startup` typically use it for "I want this to run at boot time" — not as a reactive-safety mechanism.

The failure mode is intermittent: `@PostConstruct` is safe when the first injection happens from a non-reactive context (tests, CLI, HTTP request on a worker thread). It only fails when the first injection happens under reactive load — making it a production-only bug in many setups.

## Codebase pattern

Same pattern used by `PathParserConfigurator` and `MongoPreferenceIndexes` in casehub-platform. Canonical for any bean that initialises eagerly and blocks during `@PostConstruct`.

## Why non-obvious

The standard mental model for `@ApplicationScoped` is "singleton, available everywhere, initialized once." The lazy-init detail — and its thread-safety implications in a reactive runtime — is not in the CDI spec, only in Quarkus ARC internals. A developer migrating from Spring (`@Bean` methods always run on the startup thread) would have no reason to anticipate the problem.

