The error appears in a module that has worked fine without `quarkus-work` on the classpath. Adding the dependency for an unrelated reason (needing `WorkItemLifecycleEvent`) unexpectedly breaks CDI startup. There is no documentation warning that `quarkus-work` ships a non-`@Alternative` `WorkloadProvider` that will compete with any custom implementation.

**See also:** GE-20260428-9311f8 "casehub-engine @ApplicationScoped no-op SPI beans collide with consumer implementations when engine is indexed"
## Update — 2026-06-14

**Diagnostic note:** When the "unsatisfied dependency" failure mode appears (zero WorkloadProvider candidates after excluding `JpaWorkloadProvider`), the injection that fails is `WorkItemAssignmentService`'s constructor: `WorkItemAssignmentService(WorkItemsConfig, Instance<WorkerSelectionStrategy>, WorkerRegistry, WorkloadProvider workloadProvider, ...)`. The `workloadProvider` parameter is direct injection (not `Instance<WorkloadProvider>`), so CDI cannot defer resolution — boot fails immediately.

**Test static inner class correction:** Solution 2 uses `@DefaultBean`, which is correct for standalone test source files. For `@Alternative @Priority(1)` **static inner classes** (nested inside a `@QuarkusTest` class), Quarkus auto-discovers and activates them from the current bean archive — no `quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives` entry needed. This is different from external JARs (`InMemoryWorkItemStore`), which DO require explicit `selected-alternatives`.

CDI @Nonbinding qualifier prevents multiple @Produces methods — must use InjectionPoint dispatch

### Symptom
`AmbiguousResolutionException` at Quarkus startup when two `@Produces` methods carry the same qualifier annotation with different `value()` attributes — e.g. `@Inference("splade")` and `@Inference("reranker")`.

### Context
Creating CDI producer methods for two different ONNX inference models, each identified by a qualifier annotation (`@Inference`) with a `@Nonbinding String value()`. The expectation is that `@Inference("splade")` and `@Inference("reranker")` are distinct qualifiers that route to different producers.

### What was tried
Two separate `@Produces` methods in the same class, each annotated with a different qualifier value:
```java
@Produces @Inference("splade") InferenceModel splade() { ... }
@Produces @Inference("reranker") InferenceModel reranker() { ... }
```

### Root cause
`@Nonbinding` on a qualifier's `value()` attribute means CDI ignores that attribute for bean resolution. All `@Inference` qualifiers are equivalent regardless of their value — `@Inference("splade")` and `@Inference("reranker")` resolve to the same bean type. Two producers for the same type + qualifier = ambiguity.

### Fix
Use a single `@Produces` method that accepts `InjectionPoint` and dispatches by reading the qualifier value at runtime:

```java
@Produces @Dependent @Inference("")
InferenceModel produce(InjectionPoint ip) {
    String name = ip.getAnnotated().getAnnotation(Inference.class).value();
    return switch (name) {
        case "splade" -> createSpladeModel();
        case "reranker" -> createRerankerModel();
        default -> throw new IllegalStateException("Unknown model: " + name);
    };
}
```

This is exactly the pattern used by `InferenceModelProducer` in `casehub-inference-quarkus`.

### Why this is non-obvious
The `@Nonbinding` annotation lives on the qualifier definition (in a different module), not on the producer. The error message (`AmbiguousResolutionException`) doesn't mention `@Nonbinding`. Developers unfamiliar with the CDI spec's treatment of `@Nonbinding` will try to debug the ambiguity by examining their producers — which appear distinct because the qualifier values are different.

@ApplicationScoped POJO + @Produces for same type causes CDI ambiguous bean resolution

# @ApplicationScoped POJO + @Produces for same type causes CDI ambiguous bean resolution

## Symptom

`AmbiguousResolutionException` or `UnsatisfiedDependencyException` at Quarkus build time when trying to inject a type that has both a class-level `@ApplicationScoped` annotation and a separate `@Produces @ApplicationScoped` method.

## Context

A class is annotated with `@ApplicationScoped` and has an `@Inject` constructor with parameters (e.g., plain String, int, or other primitives). The same class is also produced by a separate `@Produces @ApplicationScoped` method in a different class. The goal was to construct the class using the producer method with runtime values.

## What was tried

- Added `@Default` and `@Alternative` qualifiers — didn't resolve the ambiguity
- Tried removing `@Inject` from constructor but kept `@ApplicationScoped` — still two beans
- Checked if the producer method was being scanned — it was, and producing correctly

## Root cause

CDI sees **both** the class-level bean (via `@ApplicationScoped` component scanning) and the producer method bean as independent CDI beans of the same type.

If the class constructor has parameters CDI can't satisfy (plain Strings, ints without `@ConfigProperty`), the class-level bean fails at wiring time. Even if CDI could satisfy the constructor parameters, the presence of two beans for the same type causes `AmbiguousResolutionException` unless one is explicitly qualified.

The producer method is meant to be the **only** source of the bean, but the class-level annotations create a competing bean definition.

## Fix

Remove `@ApplicationScoped` and `@Inject` from classes that are constructed by `@Produces` methods. They should be plain POJOs — the producer owns their lifecycle and CDI registration.

```java
// Wrong — creates two beans
@ApplicationScoped
public class QdrantVectorStore {
    @Inject
    public QdrantVectorStore(String collectionName, QdrantClient client) {
        // ...
    }
}

// Elsewhere
@Produces
@ApplicationScoped
public QdrantVectorStore vectorStore() {
    return new QdrantVectorStore("my-collection", client);
}

// Correct — single bean via producer
public class QdrantVectorStore {
    // No CDI annotations on class or constructor
    public QdrantVectorStore(String collectionName, QdrantClient client) {
        // ...
    }
}

// Elsewhere
@Produces
@ApplicationScoped
public QdrantVectorStore vectorStore() {
    return new QdrantVectorStore("my-collection", client);
}
```

## Why non-obvious

The pattern feels natural: annotate the class to make it a CDI bean, AND provide a producer for custom construction. CDI's rule is that both declarations create independent beans — it doesn't recognize the producer as "replacing" the class-level bean.

Tests that construct the class directly (without a CDI container) won't catch this because they bypass bean resolution entirely. The error only appears when Quarkus builds the CDI graph.

**See also:** [GE-20260601-fcf0d9](GE-20260601-fcf0d9.md) — two @DefaultBean beans for same type → AmbiguousResolutionException | [GE-20260601-81be07](GE-20260601-81be07.md) — resolve CDI ambiguity by introducing concrete non-default bean

@DefaultBean on @Produces method makes the produced bean default — placing it on the class does not

### What you observe

Placing `@DefaultBean` on the producer class rather than the `@Produces` method makes the *producer bean itself* a default bean — but the *produced bean* (the type returned by `@Produces`) remains a regular non-default `@ApplicationScoped` bean. A consumer-provided `@ApplicationScoped` bean of the same type then conflicts, causing CDI ambiguity at boot.

### Context

Any Quarkus ARC application using a CDI producer (`@Produces`) to supply a bean that should yield automatically to consumer-provided overrides.

### What was tried

```java
// WRONG — @DefaultBean on the class makes the PRODUCER BEAN default,
// not the produced ContextDiffStrategy bean
@DefaultBean
@ApplicationScoped
class ContextDiffStrategyProducer {
    @Produces
    @ApplicationScoped
    ContextDiffStrategy produce() { ... }
}
```

With this, a consumer's `@ApplicationScoped ContextDiffStrategy` causes CDI ambiguity — the produced bean is not a default bean and does not yield.

### Root cause

`@DefaultBean` applies to the bean it directly annotates. On a producer *method*, it applies to the produced bean. On the producer *class*, it applies to the producer class bean itself — not to what the producer produces.

### Fix

Place `@DefaultBean` on the `@Produces` method, not the class:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
class ContextDiffStrategyProducer {

@Produces
    @DefaultBean         // ← on the method, not the class
    @ApplicationScoped
    ContextDiffStrategy produce() { ... }
}
```

The produced `ContextDiffStrategy` is now a default bean. Any consumer `@ApplicationScoped ContextDiffStrategy` wins automatically without configuration.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@DefaultBean` annotation is Quarkus ARC-specific (`io.quarkus.arc.DefaultBean`) and its producer-method vs producer-class placement is not explained in the Quarkus guides. The difference only surfaces as a CDI ambiguity error when a consumer tries to override the produced bean — the error doesn't point to the placement mistake.

