EventSource.addEventListener('named-event') silently drops all events when server sends unnamed SSE

## Symptom

`EventSource` handler registered with `addEventListener('mesh-update', handler)` never fires. No errors in the console. The `EventSource` connects successfully, the browser's network tab shows data arriving from the server at the expected interval, and `readyState` stays at `1` (OPEN). The handler is simply never called.

## Context

Server sends SSE responses using a framework that auto-wraps payload strings as bare `data:` fields (e.g. RESTEasy Reactive `Multi<String>` with `text/event-stream`). The client code registers a named-event listener expecting the server to tag events with a matching name.

## What was tried

Verified the EventSource URL was correct, confirmed the response headers showed `Content-Type: text/event-stream`, confirmed data was visible in the network tab. The connection looked healthy in every observable way.

## Root cause

`EventSource` has two distinct dispatch paths:

- **`source.onmessage`** — fires for SSE events that have no `event:` field, or have `event: message`
- **`source.addEventListener('name', handler)`** — fires only when the server emits `event: name\n` in the SSE frame

These do not overlap. A server that sends:
```
data: {"channels":...}

```
(no `event:` line) will trigger `onmessage` but never trigger any named-event listener. The listener is silently ignored — no error, no fallback.

## Fix

Use `source.onmessage` when the server sends unnamed SSE events:

```javascript
// ❌ Silently receives nothing if server sends unnamed events
source.addEventListener('mesh-update', e => {
    const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
    panel.update(data);
});

// ✅ Fires for unnamed events (no event: field in the SSE frame)
source.onmessage = e => {
    const data = JSON.parse(e.data);
    panel.update(data);
};
```

Only use `addEventListener('name', handler)` when you also control the server and it explicitly emits `event: name\n` in the SSE frame. In the browser SSE spec, `event: message` and no `event:` field are both handled by `onmessage`; any other value requires the matching `addEventListener`.

## Why this is non-obvious

The EventSource API looks symmetric — `addEventListener` appears to be the "proper" way to subscribe to events. The connection appears fully functional (open state, data flowing), giving no signal that the handler path is wrong. The bug is architectural: `onmessage` and named-event listeners address different event types and only one of them matches unnamed server output.

**See also:** [GE-20260617-0b0dba](GE-20260617-0b0dba.md) — renderComponent before addEventListener silently loses all connectedCallback events

Inject Event<T> in @QuarkusTest as a synchronous test driver for @Observes handlers

## Context

Writing a focused wiring test for a CDI `@Observes` handler — verifying that the handler
is correctly wired (right injection, right event routing, right downstream effect) without
going through the production event source.

## Technique

Inject `jakarta.enterprise.event.Event<T>` directly in the `@QuarkusTest` class. Construct
the event payload in the test, then call `fire()`. Since `@Observes` is synchronous CDI
(not `@ObservesAsync`), the handler completes before `fire()` returns. Assert the
downstream effect immediately (or with a short `await` if the handler triggers async engine
writes).

```java
@QuarkusTest
class SlaBreachHandlerWiringTest {

@Inject Event<SlaBreachEvent> breachEvents;
    @Inject CaseInstanceRepository caseInstanceRepository;

@Test
    void slaBreachHandler_onFail_signalsCaseContext() throws Exception {
        UUID caseId = caseHub.startCase(ctx).toCompletableFuture().get(5, SECONDS);
        assertThat(caseId).isNotNull();

// Construct and fire the event directly — bypasses ExpiryLifecycleService entirely
        var task = new BreachedTask(UUID.randomUUID(), CallerRef.encode(caseId, "human-approval"),
                                    "PR approval", Set.of("pr-leads"));
        var breach = new SlaBreachContext(BreachType.CLAIM_EXPIRED, task,
                                          Path.root(), new MapPreferences(Map.of()));
        breachEvents.fire(new SlaBreachEvent(breach, new BreachDecision.Fail("sla-breach")));

// @Observes is synchronous — handler ran before fire() returned
        // but handler calls caseHub.signal() which may trigger async engine writes
        await().atMost(5, SECONDS).pollInterval(100, MILLISECONDS).untilAsserted(() -> {
            var instance = caseInstanceRepository.findByUuid(caseId)
                    .await().atMost(Duration.ofSeconds(2));
            assertThat(instance.getCaseContext().getPath("humanApproval.status"))
                    .isEqualTo("sla-breach");
        });
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

The natural instinct is to drive the full production stack (event source → SPI → CDI event
→ handler), which works but ties the test to multiple components and introduces async
races (e.g. the production event source also triggers binding activity on the case). The
inverse pattern — injecting `Event<T>` as the *driver* rather than the *subject* — is the
opposite of how event testing is usually framed (which is: capture what you receive). It
isn't documented as a testing approach in Quarkus or CDI docs.

Related: GE-20260519-114395 covers calling `@ObservesAsync` handlers directly via the
injected CDI proxy. This entry is the synchronous `@Observes` equivalent — using `fire()`
rather than direct invocation, so the CDI dispatch mechanism is also exercised.

## Constraints

Only applies to `@Observes` (synchronous). For `@ObservesAsync`, `fire()` dispatches
off-thread and the handler may not have completed when assertions run — use
`fireAsync().toCompletableFuture().join()` or see GE-20260429-3d4e35 instead.

