Test @ObservesAsync CDI observers in @QuarkusTest with @TestTransaction + fireAsync().join()

## Test @ObservesAsync CDI observers in @QuarkusTest with @TestTransaction + fireAsync().join()

### What this is

A reliable, deterministic pattern for testing `@ObservesAsync` CDI event observers that write to a database in their own `@Transactional` context.

### How it works

An `@ObservesAsync` observer runs on a Quarkus managed executor thread with its own `@Transactional` context — separate from the test's transaction. `fireAsync()` returns a `CompletionStage` that resolves only after **all async observers have completed, including their transaction commits**. Calling `.toCompletableFuture().join()` blocks the test thread until this point, giving a precise synchronization barrier.

The test method is annotated with `@TestTransaction`, which gives the injected `EntityManager` a transaction context for reading the data the observer just committed.

```java
@QuarkusTest
class MyObserverTest {

@Inject Event<MyEvent> events;

@Inject
    @MyPersistenceUnit
    EntityManager em;

@Test
    @TestTransaction
    void observer_writesRow_andIsReadable() {
        UUID id = UUID.randomUUID();

// join() blocks until observer has committed its own transaction
        events.fireAsync(new MyEvent(id, "payload"))
              .toCompletableFuture().join();

List<MyEntity> rows = em.createQuery(
                "SELECT e FROM MyEntity e WHERE e.refId = :id", MyEntity.class)
                .setParameter("id", id)
                .getResultList();

assertThat(rows).hasSize(1);
        assertThat(rows.get(0).payload).isEqualTo("payload");
    }
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

Most developers reach for `Awaitility.await().atMost(5, SECONDS).untilAsserted(...)` to test async observers — which works but is indeterminate and adds unnecessary wait time. Few realise that `fireAsync()` returns a `CompletionStage` that resolves precisely when all observers finish (including their DB commits), making `join()` an exact synchronization point with no polling.

The `@TestTransaction` annotation on the test method is also non-obvious: without it, the directly-injected `EntityManager` may not be in a transaction context, causing `javax.persistence.TransactionRequiredException` or reading from an auto-commit connection that sees a different isolation snapshot.

**See also:** [GE-20260428-92e34e](GE-20260428-92e34e.md) — fireAsync().toCompletableFuture().join() waits until all @ObservesAsync handlers commit

**See also:** GE-20260429-da95ec "Two-bean pattern for @ObservesAsync + @Transactional with OCC retry in Quarkus"
**See also:** [GE-20260417-d67b22 — Use per-case DB query instead of shared AtomicInteger to isolate @QuarkusTest async worker assertions](GE-20260417-d67b22.md)

CDI Event.fireAsync().toCompletableFuture().join() waits until all @ObservesAsync handlers commit

## Context

Testing async CDI event pipelines where an `@ObservesAsync` handler does work (e.g. writes to a database) and you need to verify that work completed before asserting. The typical approach is `Awaitility.await().atMost(...).untilAsserted(...)` with polling — which adds latency and can be flaky.

## Technique

`Event.fireAsync()` returns a `CompletionStage<T>` that completes only after **all** `@ObservesAsync` handlers have finished — including their `@Transactional` commits. Calling `.toCompletableFuture().join()` gives deterministic, zero-polling synchronization:

```java
@Inject Event<MyEvent> events;
@Inject MyRepository repo;

@Test
@TestTransaction
void eventWritesToDatabase() {
    UUID id = UUID.randomUUID();

// Fires the event and blocks until all @ObservesAsync handlers have committed.
    // By the time join() returns, the handler's @Transactional method has committed.
    events.fireAsync(new MyEvent(id, "WorkerExecutionCompleted"))
          .toCompletableFuture()
          .join();

// Now safe to assert — no polling needed.
    List<MyRecord> results = repo.findByCase(id);
    assertThat(results).hasSize(1);
}
```

The `@ObservesAsync` handler runs in a separate thread with its own transaction context:
```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class MyEventCapture {
    @Transactional
    void onEvent(@ObservesAsync MyEvent event) {
        // This transaction commits before fireAsync()'s CompletionStage completes.
        em.persist(new MyRecord(event.id(), event.type()));
    }
}
```

## Why This Is Non-Obvious

Most developers reach for `Awaitility` + polling when testing async code. The CDI spec wording ("The returned CompletionStage ... is completed with the event") is ambiguous about whether "completed" means "dispatched" or "all observers finished". It means all observers finished — including their commits. This makes it possible to write deterministic async CDI event tests with no polling, no sleep, and no Awaitility dependency.

**See also:** [GE-20260429-3d4e35](GE-20260429-3d4e35.md) — test @ObservesAsync CDI observers with @TestTransaction + fireAsync().join() | [GE-20260429-f61708](GE-20260429-f61708.md) — CDI Event.fireAsync() inside @Transactional dispatches immediately — does NOT wait for commit

Dual-constructor CDI pattern: @Inject CDI constructor delegates to package-private test constructor accepting Consumer<T> instead of Event<T>

### Context
A `@ApplicationScoped` CDI bean fires `Event<InboundMessage>` to broadcast received messages. In `@QuarkusTest` this is fully testable via CDI observers. In pure JUnit 5 unit tests (no CDI context), `jakarta.enterprise.event.Event<T>` is an interface with ~6 abstract methods — implementing it as a test double is verbose and fragile.

### Technique
Expose a package-private constructor that accepts `Consumer<T>` instead of `Event<T>`. The CDI constructor (annotated `@Inject`) delegates to it via `this(connectors, messageEvent::fire)`:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class InboundConnectorService {

private final Consumer<InboundMessage> eventBus;

@Inject
    InboundConnectorService(@All List<InboundConnector> connectors,
                            Event<InboundMessage> messageEvent) {
        this(connectors, messageEvent::fire);   // ← delegate
    }

// Test constructor — no CDI annotation
    InboundConnectorService(List<InboundConnector> connectors,
                            Consumer<InboundMessage> eventBus) {
        this.eventBus = eventBus;
        // build registry from connectors
    }

public void receive(InboundMessage message) {
        eventBus.accept(message);   // fires in prod, records in tests
    }
}
```

Unit tests call the package-private constructor directly:
```java
List<InboundMessage> captured = new ArrayList<>();
InboundConnectorService service =
    new InboundConnectorService(List.of(), captured::add);
service.receive(msg);
assertThat(captured).containsExactly(msg);
```

### Why this is non-obvious
The obvious alternatives — implementing `Event<T>` as a mock or using Mockito — require either verbose boilerplate (6+ interface methods) or a mocking framework dependency in production code. `Consumer<T>` already exists in `java.util.function` and is exactly the shape needed: one method, one type parameter. The `this(...)` delegation pattern keeps the CDI constructor thin and avoids duplicating setup logic between the two constructors.

### Alternatives considered
- `@QuarkusComponentTest` — can test a single CDI bean in isolation, but requires Quarkus augmentation and is slower than a plain JUnit 5 test
- Mockito `mock(Event.class)` — works but adds a test framework dependency and makes test intent less clear; mock returns null by default for `fireAsync()`
