CDI @Qualifier + AnnotationLiteral producer for configurable named resource in Quarkus extensions

## Context

A Quarkus extension needs to inject a named CDI resource (EntityManager, DataSource, etc.) where the name comes from extension config, not hardcoded. The consuming app may have only a named persistence unit, only the default, or both.

## Technique

1. **Define a custom CDI qualifier** for the extension's internal injection points:

```java
@Qualifier
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Target({ElementType.FIELD, ElementType.METHOD, ElementType.PARAMETER, ElementType.TYPE})
public @interface LedgerPersistenceUnit {}
```

2. **Write a producer** that reads config and selects the right bean via `Instance<T>` + `AnnotationLiteral`:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class LedgerEntityManagerProducer {

@Inject LedgerConfig config;

@Produces @LedgerPersistenceUnit
    public EntityManager produce(@Any Instance<EntityManager> instance) {
        String pu = config.datasource().orElse("").trim();
        if (pu.isBlank()) {
            return instance.select(Default.Literal.INSTANCE).get();
        }
        return instance.select(new PersistenceUnitLiteral(pu)).get();
    }

// AnnotationLiteral for @PersistenceUnit — creates a runtime qualifier instance
    @SuppressWarnings("ClassExplicitlyAnnotation")
    private static final class PersistenceUnitLiteral
            extends AnnotationLiteral<PersistenceUnit> implements PersistenceUnit {
        private final String value;
        PersistenceUnitLiteral(String value) { this.value = value; }
        @Override public String value() { return value; }
    }
}
```

3. **Update all extension injection points** to use the custom qualifier:

```java
@Inject @LedgerPersistenceUnit EntityManager em;
```

4. **Consumers configure via properties** — no code changes needed:

```properties
# Only needed if the app has no default persistence unit
quarkus.ledger.datasource=mydb
```

## Why this is non-obvious

The instinctive fix is to add the consumer's qualifier directly to the extension's beans, which couples the extension to one consumer. The qualifier + producer indirection is the standard CDI pattern for this problem, but it's easy to overlook because `AnnotationLiteral` is rarely used outside framework code.

The custom qualifier also makes the injection IntelliJ-stable — IDEs treat standard `@Inject` + `@YourQualifier` correctly and never strip the import, unlike `@PersistenceUnit("hardcoded-name")` which IDE import optimizers sometimes remove as unused.

### Why this fix

The qualifier is owned by the extension and has no external name — consumers never see or configure it. All configurability flows through the extension's own config key. This keeps the extension's CDI wiring self-contained.

**See also:** [GE-20260424-e33d79](GE-20260424-e33d79.md) — hardcoding consumer-specific @PersistenceUnit breaks all other consumers; this technique is the fix
**See also:** GE-20260427-543663 — @Produces @DefaultBean for library-level overridable CDI defaults

@Alternative @Qualifier on the bean class restricts CDI injection to qualifier-scoped points — prevents ambiguity with plain interface injection

### What a skilled developer would do instead

Place the CDI qualifier only on the injection point (`@Inject @CrossTenant CrossTenantChannelStore`). This is the canonical CDI usage. When an `@Alternative @Priority(1)` bean is added to the same interface, developers either add `@Any` to the injection point or use `@Produces` to disambiguate — both requiring changes to consumer code.

### The technique

Place the CDI qualifier on the **bean class itself** (not just on injection points). The qualifier becomes part of the bean's type set — the alternative only satisfies injection points bearing that qualifier, leaving plain `@Default` injection points unresolved (correctly):

```java
// Bean class carries the qualifier — restricts what injection points it satisfies
@Alternative
@Priority(1)       // globally enabled in @QuarkusTest contexts
@ApplicationScoped
@CrossTenant       // ← qualifier on the CLASS, not just an injection point
public class InMemoryCrossTenantChannelStore implements CrossTenantChannelStore {
    // ...
}
```

Without `@CrossTenant` on the class, this `@Alternative @Priority(1)` bean would satisfy ALL injection points of type `CrossTenantChannelStore` — including unqualified ones — causing ambiguity or incorrect wiring.

With it, only `@Inject @CrossTenant CrossTenantChannelStore` resolves to this bean. Plain `CrossTenantChannelStore` injection points remain unresolved (as intended if no default exists) or resolve to a different bean.

### Why this is non-obvious

CDI qualifiers are almost always discussed in the context of injection points, not bean declarations. The CDI spec does allow qualifiers on bean classes, but the typical mental model is "qualifier narrows *where* a bean is injected" not "qualifier restricts *which* injection points the bean can satisfy." The distinction matters when:

- You have two beans implementing the same interface — one tenant-filtered, one cross-tenant
- You want the cross-tenant version to satisfy ONLY explicitly-qualified injection points
- `@Alternative @Priority(1)` would otherwise satisfy all injection points of the type, overriding the tenant-filtered bean everywhere

### When to use

Use when:
- You have a producer method or `@Alternative` that should only wire to explicitly-qualified injection points
- The same interface has both a "default scoped" and a "privilege-scoped" implementation (e.g. tenant-filtered vs cross-tenant, or authenticated vs system-actor)
- Multiple injection points exist and you want exactly one category to use the alternative

### Verified on

Quarkus 3.32.2, CDI 4.0 (Jakarta EE 10).

**See also:** [GE-20260607-c39651](GE-20260607-c39651.md) — Quarkus CDI: custom @Qualifier on a bean must also appear on direct type-safe injection points | [GE-20260515-fd3156](GE-20260515-fd3156.md) — @DefaultBean on @Produces method | [GE-20260604-2f0889](GE-20260604-2f0889.md) — @Alternative @Priority stubs appear in @All List alongside original

@Produces @DefaultBean for library-level overridable CDI defaults without @Alternative

### Context
A library module provides a default CDI bean (e.g. a `HolidayCalendar` implementation). Application developers should be able to override it with their own implementation without fighting with `@Alternative @Priority(1)` boilerplate.

### Technique
Use `@Produces @DefaultBean` on a producer method in the library:

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class HolidayCalendarProducer {

@Inject WorkItemsConfig config;

@Produces
    @DefaultBean          // ← yields to any @ApplicationScoped HolidayCalendar bean
    @ApplicationScoped
    public HolidayCalendar produce() {
        return config.businessHours().holidayIcalUrl()
                .filter(url -> !url.isBlank())
                .map(ICalHolidayCalendar::new)
                .map(HolidayCalendar.class::cast)
                .orElseGet(() -> new ConfigHolidayCalendar(config));
    }
}
```

Application override (no annotations needed beyond the scope):

```java
@ApplicationScoped
public class CompanyHolidayCalendar implements HolidayCalendar {
    @Override
    public boolean isHoliday(LocalDate date, ZoneId zone) {
        return /* query HR system */ false;
    }
}
```

The application bean wins automatically — `@DefaultBean` makes the producer act as a fallback only when no other bean satisfies the injection point.

### Why this is non-obvious
Most developers reach for `@Alternative @Priority(1)` on the overriding bean, which requires the application to know about ArC's priority system. `@DefaultBean` inverts this: the library marks its own bean as "low priority" and the application doesn't need any special annotations. It's a Quarkus ArC feature (`io.quarkus.arc.DefaultBean`), not in the CDI spec — easy to miss.

### Constraints
Quarkus only (ArC extension). `@DefaultBean` is not available in standard CDI containers.

