Visitor/accumulator pattern for thread-safe multi-backend aggregation — typed methods replace shared response objects

## The Technique

When assembling a response from N independent backends that run concurrently, define a typed visitor/accumulator interface rather than a shared mutable response object. Contributors call typed accumulator methods (`acc.trustScore()`, `acc.workItem()`, `acc.commitment()`) instead of returning objects to be merged. The accumulator implementation is thread-safe by construction.

```java
// Interface (stdlib types only — no domain objects)
public interface ActorStateAccumulator {
    void trustScore(Double score);               // null = unscored
    void workItem(UUID id, String title, ...);
    void commitment(UUID id, UUID channelId, ...);
}

// Thread-safe implementation
class ActorStateAccumulatorImpl implements ActorStateAccumulator {
    private final AtomicReference<Double> trustScore = new AtomicReference<>(null);
    private final CopyOnWriteArrayList<WorkItemSummary> workItems = new CopyOnWriteArrayList<>();
    private final ConcurrentHashMap<String, String> warnings = new ConcurrentHashMap<>();
    // ... typed methods populate fields ...
}

// Aggregator runs contributors concurrently
contributors.forEach(c -> CompletableFuture.runAsync(() -> {
    try {
        c.contribute(actorId, accumulator);
        accumulator.markSucceeded(c.sourceName());
    } catch (Exception e) {
        accumulator.markFailed(c.sourceName(), ...);
    }
}, managedExecutor));
CompletableFuture.allOf(futures).join();
return accumulator.build();
```

## Why Non-obvious

The natural approach is to make each contributor return a partial `Map<String, Object>` or a typed partial response, then merge them. This requires either synchronization on the merge step or collecting all futures before touching shared state. The visitor approach is cleaner: contributors never see each other's data; the accumulator encapsulates all thread-safety; and the interface becomes the contract for what data each backend can contribute — no `instanceof` checks, no map key strings.

## Why This Fix

The typed interface also enforces null contracts at the API level (`trustScore(Double)` accepting null is explicit; `trustScore(double)` would prevent it). The accumulator's `build()` method runs on a single thread after all contributors finish, making response assembly simple and safe.

## Constraints

Works best when contributors are truly independent (different backends, different transaction contexts). If contributors must share data with each other, the pattern breaks down.

ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfPresent returning null atomically removes the map entry — eliminates TOCTOU in prune-if-empty patterns

### What

`ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfPresent(key, fn)` removes the map entry atomically when the function returns `null`. The function executes under the map's internal per-bucket lock, so the condition check and the entry removal are a single atomic operation — no separate lock or CAS loop required.

### Context

Replacing the two-step "remove item from collection value, then remove map entry if empty" pattern that has a TOCTOU race window (see GE-20260522-98b286 for the gotcha this technique resolves):

```java
// WRONG — TOCTOU between isEmpty() and remove()
list.remove(item);
if (list.isEmpty()) map.remove(key, list);
```

### Technique

```java
// CORRECT — atomic collapse of remove + prune under map segment lock
map.computeIfPresent(key, (k, list) -> {
    list.remove(item);
    return list.isEmpty() ? null : list;
});
```

The lambda:
1. Runs exclusively (per-bucket lock held for the duration)
2. Mutates the list value
3. Returns `null` → entry is atomically removed
4. Returns the list → entry is atomically kept

No window exists between the empty-check and the removal for a concurrent thread to add to the list.

### Why This Is Non-Obvious

The Javadoc for `computeIfPresent` focuses on the update-in-place use case. The `null`-return semantic — that it atomically removes the entry — is documented but easy to miss. Most developers encountering the TOCTOU race would reach for a `synchronized` block on the map or a per-key `ReentrantLock`, which adds significantly more complexity and contention surface. The `computeIfPresent`/`null` pattern is zero-overhead (same cost as the existing remove path), requires no auxiliary lock, and expresses the intent clearly in four lines.

### Why This Fix

`synchronized(map)` would work but serialises all operations on all keys globally. A `ConcurrentHashMap<K, ReentrantLock>` of per-key locks works but adds allocation and lookup overhead. `computeIfPresent` already holds the per-bucket lock — returning `null` is the natural way to express "conditionally remove" within that lock without widening the critical section.

Panache.withTransaction() uses the default persistence unit — silently wrong in apps with only a named PU

## Symptom

`IllegalStateException: No current Mutiny.Session` or `org.hibernate.SessionException` when a reactive service method is called from within a consumer app that has only a named datasource configured (no default datasource). The service works in the library's own tests (which configure a default datasource) but fails in the consumer.

## Context

A library's reactive service uses `Panache.withTransaction(() -> { ... })` (the static, zero-argument form). The consuming app configures a named datasource only:

```properties
# Consumer app — named datasource only, no default
quarkus.datasource.qhorus.db-kind=h2
quarkus.datasource.qhorus.reactive.url=...
```

The library tests configure a default datasource and pass. Consumer integration fails.

## Root cause

`Panache.withTransaction(Supplier<Uni<T>>)` (zero-argument form) always uses the **default** persistence unit. When a consumer app has no default PU, Panache cannot acquire a session and throws at runtime. The library's own tests define a default datasource (usually H2 in test config), so the problem is invisible until a consumer app is built.

## Fix

Use the named-PU overload `Panache.withTransaction(String persistenceUnitName, Supplier<Uni<T>>)`:

```java
// Before — uses default PU, breaks in consumers with named-PU-only config
return Panache.withTransaction(() -> {
    return messageStore.put(message);
});

// After — explicitly targets the named PU
return Panache.withTransaction("qhorus", () -> {
    return messageStore.put(message);
});
```

Apply consistently to all transactional methods in the library — if one method uses the named form and another uses the default form, intermittent failures result depending on which code path the consumer exercises first.

## Why this is non-obvious

The zero-argument `withTransaction()` is the documented primary form. The named-PU overload is not prominently documented. The failure only appears in consumers that don't configure a default datasource — the library's own tests pass, giving false confidence. The `IllegalStateException` message ("No current Mutiny.Session") doesn't mention the PU name mismatch.

**See also:** [GE-20260512-ea776c](GE-20260512-ea776c.md) — Quarkus named persistence units silently skip schema generation

**See also:** [GE-20260530-da427e](GE-20260530-da427e.md) — Quarkus multi-PU sub-package matching assigns LedgerEntry subclass entities to wrong PU

**See also:** [GE-20260612-8e925b](GE-20260612-8e925b.md) — Quarkus quarkus.hibernate-orm.<pu-name>.sql-load-script for named PU

**See also:** [GE-20260618-08cb96](GE-20260618-08cb96.md) — Named Hibernate ORM PU requires both @PersistenceUnit qualifier AND packages config

