Config-driven CDI strategy selection: Instance<T>.select(NamedLiteral.of(name)).get() in @PostConstruct

## What it is

Select a CDI bean by name at startup from a config property using `Instance<T>` and `NamedLiteral`. Zero framework code beyond `@Named` on each implementation.

## When to use it

When you have multiple implementations of an interface, want to select one via config (`application.properties`), and want the selection to happen once at startup with fast-fail if the name is wrong.

## The pattern

```java
// Interface
public interface KiteStrategy {
    Point2d retreatTarget(Unit unit, List<Unit> enemies);
}

// Implementations
@ApplicationScoped @Named("direct")
public class DirectKiteStrategy implements KiteStrategy { ... }

@ApplicationScoped @Named("terrain-aware")
public class TerrainAwareKiteStrategy implements KiteStrategy { ... }

// Selector bean
@ApplicationScoped
public class TacticsController {

@Inject
    @ConfigProperty(name = "quarkmind.tactics.kite.strategy", defaultValue = "direct")
    String kiteStrategyName;

@Inject Instance<KiteStrategy> kiteStrategies;

private KiteStrategy kiteStrategy;

@PostConstruct
    void init() {
        kiteStrategy = kiteStrategies.select(NamedLiteral.of(kiteStrategyName)).get();
    }
}
```

Config:
```properties
quarkmind.tactics.kite.strategy=terrain-aware
```

## Why this is non-obvious

Most developers reach for a factory class, a switch statement, or `@Alternative` + `@Priority`. The `Instance<T>.select(NamedLiteral.of(name))` idiom is in the CDI spec but rarely shown in examples. Key benefits:
- **Fast fail at startup** — `get()` throws `UnsatisfiedResolutionException` immediately if the name doesn't match any `@Named` bean, not silently at call time
- **Zero factory boilerplate** — CDI discovers all implementations automatically; adding a new strategy is just a new `@Named` class
- **Future-extensible** — a `@Named("lua") LuaStrategy` implementation can be added without touching the selector

`NamedLiteral.of(name)` is from `jakarta.enterprise.inject.literal.NamedLiteral` (CDI 4.x / Quarkus 3.x).

**See also:** GE-20260421-566d3d (self-registering strategy registry via @Any Instance<T> + name() for O(1) lookup)

@Alternative @Priority(10) CDI test bean avoids Quarkus CDI restart caused by @InjectMock

## The technique

When you need to substitute a CDI bean for ALL test classes (not per-method), use a test-only `@Alternative @Priority(10) @ApplicationScoped` CDI bean registered in `quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives` in test `application.properties`. This approach:

1. Does NOT change the CDI profile between test classes (no Quarkus restart)
2. The bean is active for the entire test run — all classes share the same substitution
3. The test bean can be request-aware — inspect inputs to return different responses per test case

```java
// src/test/java/com/example/TestMyChatModelProvider.java
@Alternative
@Priority(10)
@ApplicationScoped
public class TestMyChatModelProvider extends MyChatModelProvider {

@Override
    public ChatModel get() {
        return new ChatModel() {
            @Override
            public ChatResponse doChat(ChatRequest request) {
                // Examine the request to decide which response to return
                String text = extractUserText(request);
                if (text.contains("error-trigger")) {
                    return errorResponse();
                }
                return successResponse();
            }
        };
    }
}
```

```properties
# test/resources/application.properties
quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives=\
  ...,\
  com.example.TestMyChatModelProvider
```

## When to use

Use this when:
- Multiple test classes need the substitution (not just one)
- The bean's CDI profile change would force a Quarkus restart (e.g. beans used in `@ApplicationScoped` lazy-init paths)
- The substitution behavior needs to be request-aware (different responses for different inputs)

Use `@InjectMock` only when:
- A single test class needs a unique mock behavior
- The bean is NOT involved in any lazy-init path that bakes values at augmentation time
- No other `@QuarkusTest` class runs in the same Quarkus instance with a conflicting profile

## Why this is non-obvious

Most Quarkus documentation recommends `@InjectMock` as the standard test double mechanism. The CDI-restart consequence of mixing `@InjectMock` and non-mocked test classes in the same JVM is not documented. The `@Alternative + selected-alternatives` approach is documented as a production CDI pattern, not as a testing technique.

**See also:** [GE-20260618-c552c3](GE-20260618-c552c3.md) — @InjectMock on @ApplicationScoped triggers CDI restart → Vert.x codec double-registers (the problem this technique solves) | [GE-20260608-983041](../jvm/GE-20260608-983041.md) — CDI @Alternative @Priority on subclass of framework-internal @ApplicationScoped | [GE-20260429-a79d0e](../jvm/GE-20260429-a79d0e.md) — @Alternative @Priority auto-activates without quarkus.arc.selected-alternatives | [GE-20260512-c246b0](../jvm/GE-20260512-c246b0.md) — Test Quarkus CDI SPI implementations with @Alternative static inner classes

@ApplicationScoped CDI bean captures immutable-record snapshot at construction — registry updates are invisible

## @ApplicationScoped CDI bean captures immutable-record snapshot at construction — registry updates are invisible

**ID:** GE-20260604-b561df
**Stack:** Quarkus CDI, Java 21 (records)
**Labels:** `#cdi` `#applicationscoped` `#registry` `#snapshot` `#stale-state`

### Symptom

An `@ApplicationScoped` CDI bean holds a `final ImmutableRecord session` field. External callers update a backing registry (e.g. `ConcurrentHashMap.computeIfPresent(id, s -> s.withField(newValue))`) which replaces the map entry with a new record instance. The bean's `session` field still points to the original snapshot — silently. No exception, no warning. Any data derived from the snapshot (e.g. selection context) remains perpetually stale.

### Context

Pattern: a factory or lifecycle manager constructs a CDI bean and passes in an immutable record at construction time. The record is also stored in a registry keyed by an ID. Later, update operations target the registry entry (replacing the map value) rather than the bean's field.

Occurs whenever:
- The bean is `@ApplicationScoped` (constructed once)
- The constructor argument is an immutable record (or any value type)
- The registry stores the canonical, live value but the bean holds its own copy

### What was tried

The registry `updateSelection()` call was verified to work: `computeIfPresent` did replace the map entry with a new record carrying the updated selection. The bug was not in the registry — it was that the already-constructed CDI bean had captured the pre-update snapshot and never re-read from the registry.

### Root cause

CDI `@ApplicationScoped` beans are proxied and constructed once. A constructor parameter (or field set during construction) is a snapshot of the value at that moment. Java immutable records cannot be mutated in-place. When the registry replaces its entry, the bean holds a stale reference to the previous record. There is no observer mechanism to notify the bean that the registry entry was replaced.

### Fix

Inject the registry itself (not the record) and resolve the live value on each method invocation:

```java
// Before — captures a snapshot at construction; update_selection changes are invisible
public class ReviewerChannelBackend implements ChannelBackend {
    private final ReviewSession session;  // stale after any registry update

ReviewerChannelBackend(ReviewSession session, ...) {
        this.session = session;  // snapshot — never updated again
    }

public void post(...) {
        String context = buildSelectionContext(this.session);  // always stale
    }
}

// After — reads live session from registry on every invocation
public class ReviewerChannelBackend implements ChannelBackend {
    private final ReviewSessionRegistry registry;
    private final UUID channelId;

ReviewerChannelBackend(ReviewSessionRegistry registry, UUID channelId, ...) {
        this.registry = registry;
        this.channelId = channelId;
    }

public void post(...) {
        ReviewSession session = registry.find(channelId).orElse(null);
        if (session == null) return;  // session ended — silently ignore
        String context = buildSelectionContext(session);  // always live
    }
}
```

### Why this is non-obvious

The registry update (`computeIfPresent`) is correct and the map entry is genuinely replaced. The bug is not in the update path — it's in the consuming bean having captured the old value. This looks like the selection update "didn't work" rather than "worked but the result was never read." The indirection through a CDI proxy obscures when construction happened. Expert Quarkus developers know that `@ApplicationScoped` beans are singletons but don't instinctively reach for "is my injected value a stale snapshot?" when debugging.

### Why inject registry over record

Injecting the registry (and resolving on each call) ensures the consumer always sees the canonical live value. The overhead is a single map lookup per invocation — negligible for event-driven handlers. Passing the record directly at construction is convenient but creates invisible coupling between the construction-time state and the consuming bean's view of the world.

*Score: 11/15 · Included because: completely silent failure that looks like a feature isn't working; expert CDI developers would reach for injection patterns without instinctively suspecting snapshot capture; the fix is non-obvious (must restructure the constructor) · Reservation: the CDI singleton-per-context rule is well-known; the missing piece is applying it to snapshot semantics specifically*

CDI @PostConstruct JPA calls fail silently on Vert.x IO thread when engine's registerKnownDefinitions triggers lazy bean init

### Symptom

Quarkus startup fails with `BlockingNotAllowedException` or `ContextNotActiveException` from a `@PostConstruct` method that calls `ChannelService.findByName()` (JPA/Panache). The stack trace shows the call originates from `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry.registerKnownDefinitions()` running on a Vert.x IO thread.

### Context

When `QuarkMindCaseHub extends CaseHub` is discovered by CDI, the engine's `DefaultCaseDefinitionRegistry` calls `registerKnownDefinitions()` on a Vert.x IO thread during startup event processing. This triggers lazy resolution of CDI beans injected by the CaseHub subclass (e.g. `Instance<TaskDefinition>`). If any of those beans have `@PostConstruct` methods that perform blocking JPA work (channel creation, preference loading), the call hits the Vert.x IO thread blocker.

### Root cause

Vert.x IO threads prohibit blocking operations. JPA/Panache calls are blocking. The engine's case definition registration happens on an IO thread, which transitively constructs `@ApplicationScoped` beans that were designed to initialize eagerly via `@PostConstruct`.

### Fix

Defer blocking work from `@PostConstruct` to lazy initialization with double-checked locking:

```java
// Before (fails on IO thread):
@PostConstruct
void init() {
    channel = channelService.findByName("my-channel")
        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...));
}

// After (lazy, thread-safe):
private volatile UUID channelId;

private void ensureChannel() {
    if (channelId == null) {
        synchronized (this) {
            if (channelId == null) {
                channelId = QuarkusTransaction.requiringNew()
                    .call(() -> channelService.findByName("my-channel")
                        .orElseGet(() -> channelService.create(...)))
                    .id();
            }
        }
    }
}
```

Call `ensureChannel()` on first use (e.g. first `execute()` call) instead of in `@PostConstruct`.

### Why this is non-obvious

The `@PostConstruct` pattern is standard for CDI bean initialization. It works for all non-JPA work. The failure only occurs when the bean is transitively discovered by the engine's CaseDefinitionRegistry — a dependency chain that crosses module boundaries and isn't visible from the bean's own code.

Two @Startup @ApplicationScoped Quarkus beans have no guaranteed initialization order — @PostConstruct side effects are unreliable across beans

### Symptom

Bean A's `@PostConstruct` sets a shared global (e.g. `Path.setDefaultParser()`). Bean B's `@PostConstruct` calls code that depends on that global. Works in some environments, fails silently or intermittently in others because the order in which Quarkus initialises `@Startup` beans is not defined.

### Context

Any Quarkus application with two or more `@Startup @ApplicationScoped` beans where one bean's `@PostConstruct` produces a side effect (global state, static field, registry population) that another bean's `@PostConstruct` consumes.

### Root cause

CDI specifies no initialization ordering between two `@Startup @ApplicationScoped` beans. The order is implementation-defined and can vary between test and production environments. `@Startup` only guarantees that all such beans initialise before the first non-startup request — not relative to each other.

### Fix

Each `@Startup` bean reads `@ConfigProperty` directly rather than depending on another bean's `@PostConstruct` side effect. Pass the config value explicitly to helpers that need it.

```java
// BAD — depends on PathParserConfigurator.@PostConstruct having set the default parser
Path parser = Path.getDefaultParser(); // may be null or default at this point

// GOOD — read the same config key directly; CDI injects @ConfigProperty fields
// before @PostConstruct, regardless of @Startup ordering between beans
@ConfigProperty(name = "casehub.platform.path.separator", defaultValue = "/")
String pathSeparator;

@PostConstruct
void load() {
    PathParser parser = PathParser.of(pathSeparator); // explicit, no cross-bean dependency
    // ...
}
```

`@ConfigProperty` fields are always injected before `@PostConstruct` runs — that guarantee is per-bean, not cross-bean, and is reliable.

### Why this is non-obvious

`@Startup` implies "at startup" — developers assume multiple `@Startup` beans initialise in a predictable order (e.g. dependency graph or declaration order). The CDI spec guarantee is only that they initialise before the first non-startup request, not relative to each other. The failure is intermittent and environment-dependent, making it hard to reproduce reliably.

**See also:** [GE-20260607-cedf69](GE-20260607-cedf69.md) — @Startup @ApplicationScoped does not auto-invoke arbitrary methods — requires @PostConstruct

